  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hands-On Learning for Zambia&apos;&apos;s Orphans and their communities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Orphans and vulnerable children in Zambia, particularly girls, struggle to succeed in school due to poverty, HIV and neglect. They are less likely to enter school and up to 47% fail to complete their education. Numbers of such children are rising yet the Zambian Government&apos;s response is inadequate, as it lacks grass-roots consultation. This project will enable communities to address local challenges which prevent orphans and vulnerable children from getting a good education. With the help of trained workers, community committees will improve co-ordination between local support agencies. Local people will engage with those in power to show how government action could be much more effective. After three years, 6,000 orphans and vulnerable children will have improved access to quality basic education and carers will receive better services. Across 14 communities and 14 school boards, local people&apos;s voices will be heard much more effectively. Children will be able to attend improved schools, and complete their primary education successfully. This imaginative project in Zambia&apos;s Copperbelt will ensure children orphaned by HIV/AIDS receive much greater long term support into education, and encourages them to be change makers themselves within their communities.</narrative>
  </description>
